---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Start here"
	description="Yeh Bro’s blueprint for getting into the best shape of your life — mindset, training, food, sleep, supplements that matter, then optional deeper culture reading."
	canonicalPath="/start-here"
>
	<article class="prose-wrap journey">
		<p class="eyebrow">Blueprint</p>
		<h1>Start here</h1>
		<p class="lede">
			Here’s how I’d get someone into the best shape of their life — not the flashiest eight-week
			transformation, the one that still holds in twelve months. Six steps. Do them in order. Skip
			the deep culture / PED stuff until the basics feel boring in a good way.
		</p>

		<nav class="step-nav" aria-label="Journey steps">
			<a href="#step-1">1 · Mindset</a>
			<a href="#step-2">2 · Training</a>
			<a href="#step-3">3 · Food</a>
			<a href="#step-4">4 · Sleep</a>
			<a href="#step-5">5 · Supplements</a>
			<a href="#step-6">6 · Optional deeper</a>
		</nav>

		<section class="hub-block" id="step-1">
			<p class="eyebrow">Step 1</p>
			<h2>Mindset — honesty about where you’re starting</h2>
			<p>
				Be straight with yourself: training age, sleep, stress, how consistent you’ve actually been.
				Eight-week “before/after” feeds lie with lighting, pumps, filters, and sometimes pharmacology.
				Your job is a repeatable week you can run when life is average — not a hero week you abandon.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>Pick one goal for 12 weeks</strong> — get stronger, lose fat, or rebuild the habit. Not all three at once.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Measure something boring</strong> — sessions completed, body weight trend, a few lifts.
					Screenshots of peak lighting don’t count.
					<a href="/blog/training-logbook">How to read a logbook</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Ignore “natty or not” bait</strong> until your own process is solid. Comparison without
					context is how people quit.
					<a href="/blog/ego-lifting-vs-training-age">Ego lifting vs training age</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/online-coach-red-flags">Coach red flags</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/when-to-hire-in-person-coach">When to hire a coach</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/body-dysmorphia-adjacency-gym-culture">Body-image adjacency</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/tracking-weight-waist-photos">Weight vs waist vs photos</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/photo-lighting-honesty-for-lifters">Photo lighting honesty</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/sodium-water-scale-panic">Sodium / scale panic</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/gym-etiquette-without-gatekeeping">Gym etiquette</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/comment-section-ped-debate-detox">PED comment detox</a>.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Best shape of your life is a process score, not a peak still.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="hub-block" id="step-2">
			<p class="eyebrow">Step 2</p>
			<h2>Training — consistency beats complexity</h2>
			<p>
				If I were programming for most people: lift <strong>3–4 days a week</strong>, hit the big
				patterns (squat/hinge, push, pull, carry or core), and add load or reps over time. That’s
				progressive overload. Fancy splits can wait.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Simple split that works:</strong> Full body 3×/week, or Upper / Lower 4×/week.
					Keep the same lifts long enough to get good at them.
					<a href="/blog/beginner-full-body-program">Full-body template</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/upper-lower-split">Upper / lower</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/upper-lower-advanced-volume">Advanced volume</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/powerbuilding-hybrid-week">Powerbuilding hybrid</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/how-many-days-to-lift">How many days</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/home-gym-starter-minimalism">Home gym minimal</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/beginner-mistakes-home-gyms">Home gym mistakes</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/machine-only-hypertrophy-templates">Machine-only templates</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/cable-vs-free-weight-trade-offs">Cables vs free weights</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Leave 1–3 reps in the tank</strong> most sets. Destroying yourself every session is
					ego, not a plan. Progress the logbook:
					<a href="/blog/progressive-overload">Progressive overload</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/progressive-overload-for-women">Overload for women</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/rpe-reps-in-reserve">RPE / reps in reserve</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/training-logbook">Logbook</a>.
					Stuck? <a href="/blog/plateau-checklist-for-lifters">Plateau checklist</a>.
					Ramp in — <a href="/blog/warmup-before-lifting">warmup before lifting</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/warmup-circuit-you-keep">warmup circuit</a>.
					Hinges — <a href="/blog/joint-friendly-hinge-progressions">joint-friendly hinge progressions</a>.
					Pressing — <a href="/blog/barbell-vs-db-bench-shoulders">barbell vs DB bench</a>.
					Gear when it earns it — <a href="/blog/grip-belts-when-gear-helps">grip, belts, gear</a>.
					On the road — <a href="/blog/hotel-travel-gym-sessions">hotel / travel sessions</a>.
					Coming back from time off — <a href="/blog/injury-return-to-lift-mindset">injury return mindset</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Cardio:</strong> walk most days
					(<a href="/blog/walking-for-lifters">walking guide</a>). Place harder work carefully —
					<a href="/blog/cardio-on-lifting-days">cardio on lifting days</a>.
					Weekend-only lifters —
					<a href="/blog/weekend-warrior-damage-control">weekend warrior damage control</a>.
					Fried?
					<a href="/blog/deload-week">Deload</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/how-to-choose-deload-style">Deload styles</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/rest-days-vs-active-recovery">Rest days</a>.
					Slot that fits life — <a href="/blog/morning-vs-evening-training">morning vs evening</a>.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				When you’re ready for phased cut → health pivot → lean bulk education:
				<a href="/blog/bodybuilding-blueprint">Bodybuilding blueprint</a>.
			</p>
			<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Show up, progress the logbook, recover. Program-hopping is procrastination.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="hub-block" id="step-3">
			<p class="eyebrow">Step 3</p>
			<h2>Food — protein first, phases when you’re ready</h2>
			<p>
				Don’t obsess over apps before you can hit protein and roughly the right calories most days.
				Deep dive the whole rabbit hole from
				<a href="/blog/calories-and-macros-for-lifters">calories &amp; macros for lifters</a>.
				Here’s the order I’d use:
			</p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<strong>Energy balance first:</strong> what calories are, then a rough maintenance —
					<a href="/blog/what-calories-are-for-lifters">what calories are</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/estimate-maintenance-calories">estimate maintenance</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/why-am-i-not-losing-fat">why am I not losing?</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Protein:</strong> about <strong>1.6–2.2 g per kg</strong> body weight as a practical
					range for lifters.
					<a href="/blog/protein-targets-for-lifters">Protein targets</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/protein-timing-vs-total-intake">timing vs total</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/whey-vs-food-protein">Whey vs food</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/meal-structure-without-apps">Meal structure without apps</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/partner-family-food-macros">Partner / family food</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Macro split:</strong> protein → fat floor → carbs for work —
					<a href="/blog/macro-split-basics-for-lifters">macro split basics</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/carb-totals-vs-timing-for-lifters">carb totals vs timing</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/carbs-around-training">carbs around training</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/intra-carbs-for-long-sessions">intra carbs</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/dietary-fat-floors-for-lifters">dietary fat floors</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>How you hit the numbers:</strong>
					<a href="/blog/tracking-vs-hand-portions">tracking vs hand portions</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/calorie-tracking-mistakes">tracking mistakes</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/flexible-dieting-vs-meal-plans">flexible vs meal plans</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/hitting-macros-at-restaurants">restaurants / social food</a>.
				</li>
				<li><strong>Maintenance first:</strong> eat roughly enough to hold weight while training quality rises.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Deficit when ready:</strong> ~15–20% below maintenance to lose fat without torching
					lifts — <a href="/blog/cutting-without-wrecking-lifts">cutting guide</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/hunger-on-a-cut">hunger noise vs need</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/refeeds-and-diet-breaks">refeeds / diet breaks</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>After the cut:</strong> climb out with intent —
					<a href="/blog/reverse-diet-literacy">reverse diet literacy</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/cut-to-bulk-transition-mistakes">cut-to-bulk mistakes</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/recalculating-calories-after-weight-change">recalculate after weight change</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Surplus when ready:</strong> a small bump (~10%) to grow — not a dirty bulk that
					undoes the cut — <a href="/blog/lean-bulk-basics">lean bulk basics</a>.
				</li>
			</ol>
			<p>
				Phased food + training detail lives in the
				<a href="/blog/bodybuilding-blueprint-phase-1-cut">cut</a>,
				<a href="/blog/bodybuilding-blueprint-phase-2-health-pivot">health pivot</a>, and
				<a href="/blog/bodybuilding-blueprint-phase-3-lean-bulk">lean bulk</a> guides — after steps 1–4 feel normal.
			</p>
			<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Hit protein. Adjust calories slowly. Perfect macros can wait.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="hub-block" id="step-4">
			<p class="eyebrow">Step 4</p>
			<h2>Sleep / recovery — the unpaid coach</h2>
			<p>
				You can out-train a bad night once. You can’t out-train a bad month. Aim for a sleep window
				you can protect most nights — same bedtime beats heroic weekend catch-up.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					Treat <strong>7–9 hours</strong> as training support, not optional lifestyle fluff —
					<a href="/blog/sleep-for-lifters">sleep for lifters</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/sleep-hygiene-late-shift-lifters">late-shift sleep</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/sleep-trackers-useful-or-anxiety">sleep trackers</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					If resting heart rate or mood is wrecked for days, <strong>deload</strong> — cut volume
					~30% for a week instead of adding more caffeine —
					<a href="/blog/deload-week">deload week</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					Stress, steps, actual rest days, and the quiet tax from late nights matter.
					<a href="/blog/stress-and-lifting">Stress and lifting</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/walking-for-lifters">Walking for lifters</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/step-targets-without-obsessing-neat">Step targets</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/rest-days-vs-active-recovery">Rest days vs active recovery</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/alcohol-training-quiet-tax">Alcohol and training</a>.
				</li>
				<li>
					Persistent fatigue, hard cuts, or aging curiosity →
					<a href="/blog/bloodwork-for-lifters">bloodwork for lifters</a>
					(labs with a clinician — not Discord diagnosis).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Recovery is part of the program. Skip it and the logbook stalls.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="hub-block" id="step-5">
			<p class="eyebrow">Step 5</p>
			<h2>Supplements that actually matter</h2>
			<p>
				Most tubs are noise. For average lifters building the best shape of their life, the short
				list is boring on purpose:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Creatine monohydrate</strong> — 3–5 g daily. Loading optional. Form theatre is mostly marketing.
					<a href="/blog/best-creatine-australia">Creatine guide</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/creatine-myths">Myths</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/creatine-for-older-lifters">Older lifters</a> ·
					<a href="/go/creatine-amz" rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer">Muscle Nation creatine monohydrate</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Protein powder</strong> — only if food is awkward. Judge cost per ~25 g protein, not flavour ads.
					<a href="/blog/best-protein-powder-australia">Protein guide</a> ·
					<a href="/blog/whey-vs-food-protein">Whey vs food</a> ·
					<a href="/go/protein-amz" rel="sponsored noopener noreferrer">Muscle Nation whey isolate</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Caffeine</strong> — useful for training focus if you tolerate it. Not a personality.
					<a href="/blog/caffeine-dose-training">Caffeine timing</a>
					· cut-day hunger noise vs stims:
					<a href="/blog/energy-drinks-vs-pre-workout-deficit">Energy drinks vs pre in a deficit</a>
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Order of operations and what to skip:
				<a href="/blog/beginner-supplement-stack-australia">Beginner stack</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/gym-bag-basics">Gym bag basics</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/label-math-drill">Label math drill</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/omega-3-label-drill">Omega-3 label drill</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/magnesium-label-form-drill">Magnesium form drill</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/supplement-label-red-flags-australia">Label red flags</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/supplement-mistakes-beginners-australia">Beginner mistakes</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/yeh-bro-current-picks">Current picks</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Deeper when you need it:</strong>
				<a href="/blog/how-to-dose-creatine">How to dose creatine</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/when-to-take-creatine">When to take it</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/creatine-loading-phase">Loading</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/does-creatine-cause-bloating">Bloat myth</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/creatine-monohydrate-vs-hcl">Mono vs HCL</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/creapure-vs-generic-creatine">Creapure vs generic</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/best-creatine-for-beginners-australia">Creatine for beginners</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/creatine-for-women">Creatine for women</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/whey-concentrate-vs-isolate-australia">WPC vs WPI</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/lactose-whey-isolate">Lactose / isolate</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/protein-per-scoop-australia">Protein per scoop</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/protein-scoop-economics-australia">Scoop economics</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/cheapest-protein-powder-australia">Cheapest protein</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/casein-vs-whey">Casein vs whey</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/best-plant-protein-australia">Plant protein</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/best-stim-free-pre-workout-australia">Stim-free pre</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/pre-workout-without-hype">Pre without hype</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/proprietary-blend-pre-workout">Proprietary blends</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/pre-workout-vs-coffee">Pre vs coffee</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/beta-alanine-tingles">Beta-alanine tingles</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/intra-workout-electrolytes-australia">Intra electrolytes</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/magnesium-for-lifters-australia">Magnesium</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/omega-3-for-lifters-australia">Omega-3</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/vitamin-d-training-australia">Vitamin D</a> ·
				<a href="/blog/amazon-au-vs-supplement-shops">Amazon vs shops</a>.
			</p>
			<p class="takeaway"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Creatine + protein convenience cover most of the useful powder lane. Everything else earns its place later.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="hub-block" id="step-6">
			<p class="eyebrow">Step 6 · Optional</p>
			<h2>Deeper culture — when the basics are locked</h2>
			<p>
				Gym culture has a pharmacology shadow. Ignoring it creates naivety; leading with it creates
				chaos for beginners. When you’re training consistently and want literacy — risks, bloodwork,
				legality, how forums talk about phases — go here. Not day one.
			</p>
